# Everything, on one page

::::box{class=lead}
HyMarkX is small enough that its documentation fits in a single document. Every example below
is compiled by this page — the rendered half is the real component, not a screenshot.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #install}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
Install
::::

## Two commands

```sh
npm install hymarkx
```

```sh
npx hmx build index.md --out dist
```

::::box{class=lead}
`hmx` also gives you `check` for CI, `fmt`, and `dev` with live reload. Any Markdown file is
already a valid input — nothing in it has to change.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #components}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
The built-in vocabulary
::::

## Components

::::box{class=lead}
Nine components ship with the language. Each one is a container directive: three colons, a
name, optional attributes in braces, and a matching closing fence.
::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::note[Heads up]{type=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::note[Heads up]{type=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::
:::

::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::card[Quarterly report]
Revenue rose in every region except EMEA.
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::card[Quarterly report]
Revenue rose in every region except EMEA.
:::
:::

::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::metric[requests per second]
18,400
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

:::metric[requests per second]
18,400
:::
:::

::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
A release is :badge[stable]{kind=success} once the suite is green.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

A release is :badge[stable]{kind=success} once the suite is green.
:::

::::

::::box{class=lead}
The rest are `grid` for columns, `box` for a plain wrapper — give it `as=section` or `as=nav`
when the wrapper means something — and `button`, `input` and `form`, which exist so that
interactivity has native elements to attach to.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #values}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
Frontmatter and expressions
::::

## Values

::::box{class=lead}
Frontmatter is YAML, parsed with aliases bounded and custom tags disabled. Read it back with
double braces. Everything resolves at compile time, so a document that only interpolates ships
no JavaScript.
::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
---
region: EMEA
target: 12000
---

Sales in {{ region }} against a target of {{ target }}.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

Sales in EMEA against a target of 12000.
:::

::::

::::box{class=lead}
Expressions are deliberately not JavaScript. They can compare, do arithmetic, and choose
between values — and they cannot reach the network, storage, timers, or any host object. That
restriction is what makes it safe to compile a document you did not write.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #interactivity}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
State and events
::::

## Interactivity

::::box{class=lead}
Declare state in the document root, assign it from an allowed event, and read it anywhere. The
initial value is already in the HTML, so the page is correct before the runtime loads — and the
runtime is 591 bytes, emitted only when something uses it.
::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
::state{count=0}

:::button{on-click="count = count + 1"}
Increment
:::

Clicked {{ count }} times.
```
:::

:::box{class=preview}
### Rendered

A live example needs `--trust app`; this page is built in `document` mode, so the source is
shown without running it. The [gallery](/gallery#controls) and the [home page](/#how) have it
running.
:::

::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #styling}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
Scoped CSS
::::

## Styling

::::box{class=lead}
A component may carry a `<style scoped>` block. Its selectors are rewritten to match only the
elements that component emitted, and `:global(...)` opts a rule back out. Rules are emitted
once per component, however many times it is used.
::::

```md
<style scoped>
.callout { border-left: 3px solid currentColor; }
:global(body) { font-family: system-ui; }
</style>
```

::::box{class=lead}
Built-in components bring a restrained default stylesheet, and only for the components a
document actually uses. Override it with design tokens — `--hmx-color-info`, `--hmx-radius`
and the rest — rather than by fighting selectors.
::::

::::

::::box{as=section class="chapter reveal" #diagnostics}

::::box{class=eyebrow}
When something is wrong
::::

## Diagnostics

::::box{class=lead}
Nothing recoverable throws. Every problem is a diagnostic carrying a stable code, a severity and
a real span, and one renderer draws them all — so the compiler, the CLI, the language server and
the playground say the same words about the same characters.
::::

::::box{class=example}

:::box{class=pane}
### Source

```md
:::note[Heads up]{typ=warning}
Deployment starts at 8 PM UTC.
:::
```
:::

:::box{class=pane}
### What you are told

```text
warning[HMX2001]: Unknown attribute "typ" on component "note".
  ┌─ notes.hmx:1:19
  │
1 │ :::note[Heads up]{typ=warning}
  │                   ^^^
  │
help: type
```
:::

::::

::::box{class=lead}
The code is the part that lasts. `HMX2001` means the same thing in every version, so it is worth
suppressing, searching for, or asserting on in a test — while the wording is free to improve.
Severity decides what happens next: `hmx check` fails the build on an error and reports the rest.
::::

::::box{class=lead}
The [playground](/play) renders diagnostics with this same function, on whatever you type. It is
the fastest way to find out what a code means.
::::

::::

